//? Title: The Cone Algorithm in 2D: Empty Sectors and Sampling
//? Subtitle: Actual angular witnesses, the original seed-one disk and an interior-point counterexample
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
A planar point cloud can suggest an outline when some sample points have a
wide empty sector among their nearby neighbour directions. The same observation
can also arise from sparse or uneven sampling inside an object. This report
examines the library's two-dimensional largest-gap classifier, a planar analogue
of the published three-dimensional empty-cone idea. Native records expose actual
neighbours, angular witnesses and all-pairs work. We retain the original seeded
disk experiment, add hand-checkable sectors and a flagged interior counterexample,
and measure radius/threshold sensitivity. The result is a local sampling diagnostic,
with explicit numerical, sparse-neighbour and computational limits.
)

\toc

> A circular gap is an empty open sector

For query point p, collect nonzero displacement vectors to other records within
the inclusive radius r. Coincident locations have no direction and are excluded.
Repeated nonzero directions remain separate records. Sort the computed atan2
angles in [−π,π]. For k≥2 directions, the largest circular gap is

\[
G(p)=\max\left(\theta_2-\theta_1,\ldots,\theta_k-\theta_{k-1},
2\pi-(\theta_k-\theta_1)\right).
\]
\alttext(The maximum circular gap is the greatest adjacent difference in the sorted angles, including the full turn minus the first-to-last angular span.)

Each gap describes an open angular sector with no admitted neighbour ray in its
interior; neighbours on its bounding rays are allowed. The classifier flags p
when G(p)≥β, where β is a full planar opening angle in radians. With fewer than
two noncoincident directions, it uses a full-turn score by convention and flags
the query for every valid β in (0,2π]. An isolated flag therefore has a different
sampling interpretation from a densely supported outline point.

The first example compares four cardinal neighbours with a two-neighbour corner.
Both use r=1 and β=0.55π, just above a right angle. The shaded sectors come
directly from native gap_start/largest_gap witnesses, including the wrap gap.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/cone_sectors.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const long double pi=std::acos(-1.L);
  const std::vector<std::vector<dm::geometry::Point2D>> clouds{{{0,0},{1,0},{0,1},{-1,0},{0,-1}},{{0,0},{1,0},{0,1}}};
  const std::array<std::string_view,2> names{"surrounded query","corner query"};
  dm::graphics::Diagram d(110,1100);
  dm::graphics::DiagramStroke edge;edge.color={100,116,139};edge.width=1.2;
  std::print("| query fixture | admitted directions | largest gap, degrees | flagged at 0.55 pi |\n| :--- | ---: | ---: | :--- |\n");
  for(std::size_t panel=0;panel<clouds.size();++panel) {
    const auto t=dm::geometry::cone_surface_trace(clouds[panel],1,.55L*pi);
    const auto& q=t.analysis.points[0];const double cx=5.*panel;
    std::vector<dm::graphics::DiagramPoint> sector{{cx,0}};
    for(int k=0;k<=48;++k) {
      const auto angle=q.gap_start+q.largest_gap*k/48;
      sector.push_back({cx+double(std::cos(angle)),double(std::sin(angle))});
    }
    d.polygon(sector,{255,237,213},edge).ellipse({cx,0},1,1,{255,255,255,0},edge);
    for(const auto& n:t.directions[0]) {
      d.path({{cx,0},{cx+n.point.x,n.point.y}},edge)
       .ellipse({cx+n.point.x,n.point.y},.045,.045,{37,99,235},edge);
    }
    d.ellipse({cx,0},.05,.05,{15,23,42},edge)
     .text({cx,1.6},std::string(names[panel]),20)
     .text({cx,-1.65},std::format("k={}; gap {:.0f} deg; flag={}",q.neighbors,180*q.largest_gap/pi,q.surface),18);
    std::print("| {} | {} | {:.6f} | {} |\n",names[panel],q.neighbors,180*q.largest_gap/pi,q.surface);
  }
  d.text({2.5,2.7},"Actual largest open sectors, with neighbour rays on their boundaries",23)
   .text({2.5,-2.6},"Radius includes equality; threshold is the full sector angle",20)
   .save_svg("build/cone_sectors.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| query fixture | admitted directions | largest gap, degrees | flagged at 0.55 pi |
| :--- | ---: | ---: | :--- |
| surrounded query | 4 | 90.000000 | false |
| corner query | 2 | 270.000000 | true |
\image(figures/cone_sectors.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Four cardinal directions leave a 90-degree gap and do not flag the centre. East and north alone leave a 270-degree wrap gap and flag the corner query.)
\alttext(Two native unit circles show their actual maximum empty sectors in pale orange. Four cardinal neighbours leave one quarter-circle; east and north neighbours leave the other three quarters empty.)

Why does sorting suffice? Any open sector without a neighbour ray can be expanded
until its boundaries meet consecutive rays, including the cyclic first/last
pair. Thus one of these k gaps is at least as wide. Checking every gap finds
the maximum for the admitted direction set. Equal maxima keep the first strict
largest adjacent gap, with wrap tested last; equal computed angles sort by source
index. These rules make the recorded witness deterministic for a fixed input.

The published cone algorithm identifies surface particles in three dimensions
using empty spatial cones. This API instead sorts planar angles. It performs
neither that three-dimensional search nor a mesh/normal reconstruction. The two
parameters also carry this report's explicit meanings: a planar radius and a
full angular-sector width.

> A flagged point can be strictly inside the global convex hull

Place points at (1,0),(−1,0),(0,−1),(0,2), together with query (0,0). The query
is strictly inside their diamond-shaped convex hull. At r=1, the northern point
is too far away, so the admitted directions leave an empty upper half-plane:
G=π and the centre is flagged. At r=2.1 the northern direction enters and the
maximum gap drops to π/2, clearing the flag. The point and global shape did not
move; only the neighbourhood changed.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/cone_interior_counterexample.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const std::vector<dm::geometry::Point2D> p{{0,0},{1,0},{-1,0},{0,-1},{0,2}};
  const long double pi=std::acos(-1.L);
  const auto small=dm::geometry::cone_surface_analyze(p,1,.55L*pi),large=dm::geometry::cone_surface_analyze(p,2.1,.55L*pi);
  if(!small.points[0].surface || large.points[0].surface)return 1;
  std::print("| radius | centre directions | maximum gap, degrees | centre flagged | complete pair visits |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | :--- | ---: |\n");
  std::print("| 1 | {} | {:.6f} | {} | {} |\n",small.points[0].neighbors,180*small.points[0].largest_gap/pi,small.points[0].surface,small.work.pair_visits);
  std::print("| 2.1 | {} | {:.6f} | {} | {} |\n",large.points[0].neighbors,180*large.points[0].largest_gap/pi,large.points[0].surface,large.work.pair_visits);
  dm::graphics::Diagram d(120,950);
  dm::graphics::DiagramStroke edge;edge.color={148,163,184};edge.width=1.2;
  d.polygon({{-1,0},{0,-1},{1,0},{0,2}},{219,234,254,.25},edge);
  std::vector<dm::graphics::DiagramPoint> sector{{0,0}};
  const auto& q=small.points[0];
  for(int k=0;k<=48;++k) {
    const auto angle=q.gap_start+q.largest_gap*k/48;
    sector.push_back({double(std::cos(angle)),double(std::sin(angle))});
  }
  d.polygon(sector,{255,237,213},edge).ellipse({0,0},1,1,{255,255,255,0},edge);
  edge.dashed=true;d.ellipse({0,0},2.1,2.1,{255,255,255,0},edge);
  for(std::size_t i=1;i<p.size();++i)d.ellipse({p[i].x,p[i].y},.04,.04,{37,99,235});
  d.ellipse({0,0},.05,.05,{220,38,38})
   .text({0,3.1},"The centre is inside the global hull, yet flagged at radius 1",22)
   .text({1.25,2.4},"north point:\ndistance 2",18)
   .text({0,-.28},"query",18,{220,38,38})
   .text({0,-2.9},"Increasing radius to 2.1 admits the missing northern direction",20)
   .save_svg("build/cone_interior_counterexample.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| radius | centre directions | maximum gap, degrees | centre flagged | complete pair visits |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | :--- | ---: |
| 1 | 3 | 180.000000 | true | 20 |
| 2.1 | 4 | 90.000000 | false | 20 |
\image(figures/cone_interior_counterexample.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The orange sector is genuinely empty inside the smaller local cutoff, even though the query is not on the global convex-hull boundary. Local angular evidence alone cannot certify the boundary of an unknown sampled object.)
\alttext(A red centre lies inside a blue diamond. Its unit neighbourhood misses the northern point at distance two and has an empty upper semicircle. A larger dashed circle includes that northern point.)

There is a useful ideal global control. If every other point is admitted and
β=π, a gap at least π means the directions fit in a closed semicircle. Equivalently,
a line through the query supports the entire cloud on one side: the query lies
on its convex-hull boundary. The reverse implication follows from that supporting
line. This includes collinear and coincident clouds in the ambient plane. The
local cutoff deliberately changes this criterion. Finite computed angles near
a threshold are not exact geometric predicates.

> The original seeded disk, measured without claiming ground truth

Retain 2500 square candidates, radius R=10, one local mt19937 engine seeded
with one, and consecutive x/y uniform-real draws in [−R,R]. The native sampler
accepts candidates inside the disk in encounter order. On this toolchain the
entire accepted stream matches the original program; standard-library distribution
mappings are not promised bitwise identical elsewhere. Classification retains
r=2.5 and the original double-valued threshold 0.55π.

The analytic circle describes the input generator. The flags remain sample
decisions, not points proven to lie exactly on that circle. A native radial
summary measures a chosen inner disk, norm<7, and an outer band, norm>9.4.
Those cutoffs are diagnostics, rather than a universal definition of the surface.
The native equal-scale diagram keeps the known disk circular.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/cone_algorithm.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto p=dm::geometry::sample_disk_candidates(2500,10,1);
  const double threshold=.55*std::acos(-1.);
  const auto a=dm::geometry::cone_surface_analyze(p,2.5,threshold);
  const auto s=dm::geometry::radial_flag_summary(p,a.surface,7,9.4);
  if(!s.has_flagged || s.inner_flagged!=0 || a.work.pair_visits!=p.size()*(p.size()-1))return 1;
  std::print("| actual disk diagnostic | value |\n| :--- | ---: |\n| accepted points | {} |\n| flagged points | {} |\n| inner points, norm below 7 | {} |\n| inner flagged | {} |\n| outer points, norm above 9.4 | {} |\n| outer flagged | {} |\n| closest flagged radius | {:.6f} |\n| farthest flagged radius | {:.6f} |\n| ordered pair visits | {} |\n| admitted angle evaluations | {} |\n",s.point_count,s.flagged_points,s.inner_points,s.inner_flagged,s.outer_points,s.outer_flagged,s.min_flagged_radius,s.max_flagged_radius,a.work.pair_visits,a.work.angle_evaluations);
  dm::graphics::Diagram d(36,1000);
  dm::graphics::DiagramStroke point;point.width=.6;
  for(std::size_t i=0;i<p.size();++i) {
    const auto colour=a.surface[i]?dm::graphics::RGB{220,38,38}:dm::graphics::RGB{190,198,210};
    point.color=colour;const double size=a.surface[i]?.07:.04;
    d.ellipse({p[i].x,p[i].y},size,size,colour,point);
  }
  dm::graphics::DiagramStroke circle;circle.color={51,65,85};circle.width=1.2;
  d.ellipse({0,0},10,10,{255,255,255,0},circle)
   .text({0,12.5},"Original disk workload: actual local empty-sector flags",23)
   .text({0,-12.5},"Grey: not flagged; red: flagged; circle: generator radius 10",19)
   .save_svg("build/cone_algorithm.svg");
}
```
