// result_end
\caption(The actual counters distinguish complete pair work from retained direction work. Inclusive comparison can reuse a returned working gap exactly; one working ULP above it clears the query flag. Coincident locations contribute no direction.)

> Conclusion

Actual angular records explain the classifier's empty-sector test and preserve
the original dense-disk experiment. That sample's near-rim flags are useful
evidence, while the interior counterexample and parameter sweeps show why the
same local rule cannot certify an unknown physical surface. The implementation
is explicitly planar, its all-pairs cost is measured honestly, and checked
distances, coincident-point handling and bounded records make its behavior
reproducible and inspectable.

> References

- Y. Wang, S. Teitel and C. Dellago (2005), [Melting of icosahedral gold nanoclusters from molecular dynamics simulations](https://doi.org/10.1063/1.1917756), Journal of Chemical Physics 122, 214722. Published three-dimensional empty-cone idea; the library's planar angular-gap variant is distinguished above.
- Y. Wang, [Cone Algorithm: Generic surface particle identification algorithm](https://www.pas.rochester.edu/~wangyt/algorithms/cone/index.html). Author's explanation of the three-dimensional neighbourhood/cone criterion and its two adjustable geometric parameters.
